WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.070
- Today I'd like to talk about the state

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.010
of the art of autonomous vehicles,

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.840
how I see the landscape, how
others see the landscape,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.410
what we're all excited about,

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.360
ways to solve the problem

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.740
and what to look forward to in 2019

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.230
as we also get to hear from
the different perspectives

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.860
and the various leaders in
industry and autonomous vehicles

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.023
in the next few, next couple
of weeks and next few days.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.270
So the problem, the mission, the dream,

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.570
the thing that we're trying to solve

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.640
for many it may be about
entrepreneurial possibilities

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:34.830
of making money and so on.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:39.630
But really it's about
improving access to mobility,

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:40.990
moving people around in the world

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:42.910
that don't have that ability,

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.940
whether it has to do with age

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.423
or purely access of where you live.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.870
We want to increase the efficiency
of how people move about.

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.630
The ability to be productive
in the time we spend

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.920
in traffic and transportation.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.840
One of the most hated things
in terms of stress, emotion,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.730
the thing in our lives
that if we could just

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.210
with a snap of a finger remove is traffic.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.200
So the ability to convert
that into efficiency,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.230
into a productive aspect,
into a positive aspect of life

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:19.860
and really the most important
thing at least for me

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.580
and for many of us working in the space,

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.510
is to save lives, prevent
crashes that lead to injuries,

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:26.562
prevent crash that will
lead to fatalities.

00:01:26.562 --> 00:01:28.660
Here's a counter.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.530
Every 23 seconds somebody
in the world dies

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.630
in a car, auto crash.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.620
It should be a sobering, it is for me,

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.090
thing that I think about every single day.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.230
You go to bed, you wake up,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.731
you work on all the deep learning levels,

00:01:41.731 --> 00:01:43.780
all the different papers are publishing,

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.710
everything we're trying
to push forward is really

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:49.410
to save lives at the
beginning and at the end

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:50.613
that is the main goal.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.890
So with that groundwork, with
that idea, with that base,

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.460
the mission that we're all working towards

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.373
from the different ideas
and different perspectives,

00:02:00.373 --> 00:02:03.083
I would like to review
what happened in 2018.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:09.850
So first, Waymo has done incredible work

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.950
in deploying and testing their
vehicles in various domains

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.670
and having October reached the mark

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.580
of 10 million miles, German autonomously

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.270
which is an incredible accomplishment.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:26.270
It's truly a big step for
fully autonomous vehicles

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.630
in terms of deployment
and obviously is growing

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.560
and growing by day.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.210
And we'll have Drago here from Waymo

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:36.940
to talk about their work there.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.270
Then on the L2 on the
semi-autonomous side,

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:44.370
that's the pair, that's the
mirror side of this equation.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.870
The other incredible number,

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.080
that's perhaps less talked about,

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.750
is the one billion mile
mark reached by Tesla

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.477
in the semi autonomous
driving of autopilot.

00:02:56.477 --> 00:02:58.900
Now autopilot is a system
that's able to control

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.470
its position in the lane,
center itself in the lane,

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.820
it's able to control the
longitudinal movement

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.830
so not follow a vehicle

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:07.820
when there's a vehicle in front and so on.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.540
But the degree of its ability

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.540
to do so is the critical thing here,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.290
is the ability to do so
for many minutes at a time

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:18.040
even hours at a time
especially on highway driving.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.180
That's the critical thing.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.540
And the fact that they've
reached one billion

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.000
with a B miles is an
incredible accomplishment.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.370
All of that from the machine
learning perspective is data.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.203
That's data.

00:03:29.203 --> 00:03:33.200
And all of the autopilot models are driven

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.010
with the primary sensor being a camera,

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:39.710
that's computer vision.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:42.133
Now how does computer
vision work in modern day,

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.260
especially with the second
iteration of auto pilot hardware

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:47.220
there's a neural network.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.150
There's a set of neural
networks behind it.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.450
That's super exciting.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:55.010
That is probably the largest deployment

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.580
of neural networks in the world

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.500
that has a direct impact

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.670
on a human life,

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.460
that's able to decide,

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:11.000
that's able to make
life critical decisions

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.240
many times a second over and over.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:14.290
That's incredible.

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:18.780
You go from the step of image
classification on ImageNet

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.090
and you sit there with a tensor flow

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.240
and you're very happy there.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.430
You were able to achieve a 99.3 accuracy

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.010
with a state of the art algorithm.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:31.600
You take from that a step
towards there's a human life,

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.600
your parents driving, your
grandparents driving this,

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:39.440
your children driving the system

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.140
and there's a neural
network making the decision

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.150
of whether they'll live.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:47.090
So that one billion mark is
an incredible accomplishment.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:52.090
And on the sobering side and
from various perspectives,

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:56.010
the fatalities, there's
been two fatalities

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.630
that happened in March of 2018.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:00.780
One in the fully autonomous side of things

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.920
with Uber in Tempe, Arizona,
hitting a pedestrian

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.380
and leading to a pedestrian fatality.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.440
And on the semi-autonomous
side with Tesla Autopilot,

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:15.700
the third fatality that
Tesla Autopilot led to

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:20.600
and the one in 2018 is in
Mountain View, California

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.940
when Tesla slammed

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.743
into a divider killing his driver.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.360
Now the two aspects here that are sobering

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.650
and really important to think about

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:38.020
as we talk about the progression
of autonomous vehicles,

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:42.310
proliferation in our world
is our response as a public,

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.670
is from the general
public to the engineers

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:46.980
to the media and so on,

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.690
how we think about these fatalities.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.258
And obviously there's a
disproportionate amount

00:05:51.258 --> 00:05:53.510
of attention given to these fatalities.

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:54.990
And that's something as engineers

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:56.470
you have to also think about,

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:59.330
that the bar is much higher on every level

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:01.320
in terms of performance.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.830
So in order to success, as I'll argue,

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.430
in order to design successful
autonomous vehicles

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:09.440
those vehicles will have to take risks.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.430
And when the risks don't pan out,

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:17.343
the public, if the public
doesn't understand,

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.410
the general problem that we're tackling,

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:21.770
the goal of the mission,

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.170
that those risks when they don't,

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:25.830
the risks that are taken

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.680
can have significant detrimental effect

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.370
to the progress in this
autonomous vehicle space.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.510
So that's something we
really have to think about.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.780
That's our role as engineers and so on.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:37.740
Question, yeah.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.900
So the question was,
do we know the the rate

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.900
of fatalities per mile of vehicle driven

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.860
which is at the crudest level
how people think about safety.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.580
So there's about 80, 90,
100 million miles driven

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:55.310
in manually controlled
cars at every fatality.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:59.230
So one fatality per, depending
on which numbers you look at,

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.140
it's 80 to 100 million miles.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.583
In the Tesla vehicle, for example,

00:07:04.583 --> 00:07:08.500
the fatality is well we could
just take the one billion

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:09.700
and divided it by three.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:14.200
Now this, it's apples
and oranges in comparison

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.110
and that's something actually

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:16.940
that we're working on to make sure

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:19.080
that we compare it correctly.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.270
Compare the aspects of manual miles

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:26.520
that directly are comparable
to the autopilot miles.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.420
So Autopilot is a modern
vehicle that's much safer.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.100
Tesla is a modern
vehicle that's much safer

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:34.500
than the general population
of manually driven vehicles.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.340
Autopilot is driven on only
a particular kinds of roads

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:40.730
on the highway primarily,
most of the miles.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.770
The kinds of people that drive Autopilot,

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:44.590
all these kinds of factors
need to be considered

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:46.220
when you compare the two.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:48.420
But when you just look at the numbers,

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:50.650
Tesla Autopilot's three times safer

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:52.410
than manually driven vehicles.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.810
But that's not the
right way to look at it.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:58.750
And for anyone that's ever
taken a statistics class,

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:02.680
three fatalities is not, does not,

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:04.040
it's not a large number

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:07.583
by which to make any
significant conclusions.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:11.850
Nevertheless, that doesn't stop the media,

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:13.100
the New York Times and everybody

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.143
from responding to a single fatality,

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.230
which PR and marketing aspects

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:21.900
of these different companies
are very sensitive to,

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.350
which is of course troubling
and concerning for an engineer

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:26.520
that wants to save lives.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.650
But it's something that
we have to think about.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.301
Okay, 2018 in review continued.

00:08:32.301 --> 00:08:34.750
There's been a lot of announcements

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:38.710
or rather actual launches

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:42.060
of public testing of
autonomous taxi services.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:47.060
So companies that on public
roads have been delivering

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.890
real people from one location to another.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.250
Now there's a lot of caveats.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:54.580
In many of these cases
it's very small scale,

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.200
just a few vehicles, in most
cases it's very low speed,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.660
in a constrained environment,

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.020
in a constrained community

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:06.480
and almost always, really
always, with a safety driver.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.050
There's a few exceptions

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:09.240
for demonstration purposes

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.640
but there's always an
actual driver in the seat.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:13.890
Some of the brilliant folks

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:15.240
representing these companies will speak

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:20.240
in this course is Voyage doing
it in an isolated community,

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.550
awesome work they're doing
in villages in Florida,

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:26.020
Optimus Ride here in Boston doing

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:27.670
and the community in Union Point,

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:33.450
Drive.ai in Texas, May Mobility
expanding beyond Detroit

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:35.900
but really most operation's in Detroit,

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:37.560
Waymo has launched its service.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.480
Waymo one that's gotten some
publicity in Phoenix, Arizona.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.210
That Nuro doing zero occupancy deliveries

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:50.150
of groceries autonomously.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.080
So we didn't say has to
be delivering humans,

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.590
it's delivering groceries autonomously.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:57.960
Uber is quietly, or not so quietly,

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:01.900
resumed its autonomous
vehicle taxi service testing

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:06.900
in Pittsburgh in a very
careful constrained way.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.880
Aptiv, after acquiring
Carl Iagnemma and nuTonomy,

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:21.770
has been doing extensive
large-scaled taxi service testing

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:26.300
everywhere from Vegas to
Boston here to Pittsburgh

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:29.630
and in Singapore of course.

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:34.630
Aurora that spoke here last time,

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.610
the head of Tesla
Autopilot launched Aurora

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:41.870
and the Chris Urmson behind
this young upstart company

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.110
is doing testing in San
Francisco and Pittsburgh

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:47.300
and then Cruise, Kyle will
be here to talk from GM,

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:50.100
is doing testing in San
Francisco, Arizona and Michigan.

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:54.100
So when we talk about predictions,

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:56.500
I'll talk about a few people predicting

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:59.390
when we're going to
have autonomous vehicles

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:02.760
and when you yourself
think about what it means

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.870
when will they be here?

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:06.670
When will autonomous vehicles arise

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:09.220
such that that Uber that
you call will be autonomous

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.380
and not with a populated by a driver.

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:14.970
So the thing we have to think about is

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:19.220
what we think about what,
how we define autonomous,

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:21.110
what that experience looks like.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:23.780
And most importantly in these discussions,

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:25.253
we have to think about scale.

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:28.420
So we here at MIT

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:31.400
our group MIT Human
Centered Autonomous Vehicle,

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:33.890
we have a fully autonomous
vehicle that people can get in

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:36.020
if you would like and
it will give you a ride

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:37.483
in a particular location.

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:41.370
But that's one vehicle, it's not a service

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:43.690
and it only works on particular roads.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:45.250
It's extremely constrained.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.940
In some ways it's not much different

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:50.120
than most of the companies that
we were talking about today.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:52.830
Now scale here, there's a magic number,

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.330
I'm not sure what it is but for this,

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:57.500
the purpose of this conversation
let's say it's 10,000,

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:59.810
where there's a meaningful deployment,

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:04.660
when it's truly going beyond
that prototype demo mode

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:06.360
to where everything is under control,

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.350
to where it's really touching
the general population

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:11.030
in a fundamental way.

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:13.480
Scale is everything here and it starts,

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.010
let's say at 10,000.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:16.240
Just to give you for reference,

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:19.480
there's 46,000 active Uber
drivers in New York City.

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:22.160
So that's what 10,000 feels like some,

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:26.290
you know 25, 30 % of the
Uber drivers in New York City

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:30.710
all of a sudden are become passengers.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:38.640
So the predictions, I'm
not a marketing PR person,

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.460
so I don't understand what everybody has

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.720
to have make a prediction
but they all seem to.

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:45.750
Although major automakers
have made a prediction

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.350
of when they'll have a deploy,

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:50.283
when they will be able to
deploy autonomous vehicles.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.903
Tesla has made in early 2017,

00:12:56.750 --> 00:13:00.393
a prediction that it will
have autonomous vehicles 2018.

00:13:00.393 --> 00:13:04.280
In 2018 they've now adjusted
the prediction to 2019.

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:09.280
Nissan, Honda, Toyota have
made prediction for 2020

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:12.180
under certain constraints
in highway urban.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:16.190
Hyundai and Volvo has in 2021.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:20.330
BMW and Ford, Ford saying at scale,

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:23.560
so a large scale deployment 2021.

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:27.740
And Chrysler in '21 and Daimler
saying in the early '20s.

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:31.360
So there is the the predictions

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.250
that are extremely optimistic
that are perhaps driven

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:41.360
by the instinct that the
company has to declare

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:44.260
that they're at the
cutting edge of innovation.

00:13:44.260 --> 00:13:46.750
And then there is many
of the leading engineers

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:50.730
behind the leading these
teams including Carl Iagnemma

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:55.730
and Gill Pratt from MIT
who in injects a little bit

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:01.190
of caution and grounded

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:05.360
ideas about how difficult it is

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.700
to remove the human from
the loop of automation.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:11.780
So Carl says that basically teleoperation,

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.850
kind of gives this analogy of an elevator

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:16.150
and the elevators fully autonomous

00:14:16.150 --> 00:14:18.710
but there is still a
button to call for help

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:20.360
if something happens.

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:22.830
And that's how he thinks
about autonomous vehicles.

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:25.070
Even with greater and
greater degree of automation,

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:27.380
they're still going to have
to be a human in the loop,

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:28.650
they're still going to be a way

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.280
to contact a human to get help.

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:34.040
And Gill Pratt and Toyota

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.340
and they're making some
announcements at CES,

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:37.450
basically saying that the human

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:39.130
in the loop is the fundamental aspect

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:41.399
that we need to approach this
problem and removing the human

00:14:41.399 --> 00:14:45.113
from consideration is
really, really far away.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:49.340
And Gill, who's historically
and currently is one

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:51.840
of the sort of the great
roboticists in the world

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.930
that defined a lot of the DARPA challenges

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:56.900
and a lot of our progress
historically speaking

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:58.350
up to this point.

00:14:58.350 --> 00:14:59.800
So they're really the full spectrum,

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:04.060
we can think of it as
the Elon Rodney spectrum

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:07.390
of optimism versus pessimism.

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:11.610
Elon Musk, who's extremely
bold and optimistic

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:13.490
about his predictions.

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:17.210
I often connect with this kind of thinking

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:18.390
because sometimes you have to believe

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:21.830
the impossible is possible
in order to make it happen.

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:24.000
And then there is Rodney,

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.820
also one of the great roboticists,

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:30.600
the former head of the of
CSAIL, the AI laboratory here,

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:33.040
is a little bit on the pessimistic side.

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:34.010
So for Elon,

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:37.210
now fully autonomous
vehicle will be here in 2019

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:39.970
for Rodney the vehicles are really,

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:43.200
fully autonomous are beyond 2050.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:46.450
But there, he believes in the '30s

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:51.090
there will be a significant,
a major city will be able

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:56.070
to allocate a significant
region of that city

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.570
where manual driving is fully banned.

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:00.470
Which is the way he believes

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:02.840
those vehicles could, autonomous
vehicle really proliferate

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:06.080
when you ban manually driven
vehicles in certain parts.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.440
And then in the '40s, 2045 or beyond,

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:13.620
majority of U.S cities will
ban manually driven vehicles.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:18.620
Of course the quote from
Elon Musk in 2017 is that,

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:22.600
my guess is that in probably 10 years

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:25.610
it will be very unusual
for cars to be built

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:27.360
that are not fully autonomous.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:32.360
So we also have to think about
the long tail of the fact

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:33.960
that many people drive cars

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:36.110
that are 10 years old, 20 years old.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:39.860
So even when you have every
car's built as fully autonomous,

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:41.350
it's still gonna take time

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:44.390
for that dissipation
of vehicles to happen.

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:47.363
And so my own view beyond predictions,

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:51.450
to take a little pause into the ridiculous

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:53.613
and the fun to explain the view.

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.930
Yes that is me playing guitar
in our autonomous vehicle.

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:03.550
Now the point of this ridiculous
video and embarrassing,

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:04.950
I should've never played it.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.950
Yeah, okay, I think
it's gonna be over soon.

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:12.699
Now for those of you born in
the '90s that's classic rock.

00:17:12.699 --> 00:17:15.000
(audience laughing)

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.780
So the point I'm trying to
make beyond predictions is

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:23.330
that autonomous vehicles will
not be adopted by human beings

00:17:23.330 --> 00:17:25.800
in the near term, in the next 10-15 years,

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:27.390
because they're safer.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:31.170
Safety is not going to, they
may be safer but that is,

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:33.593
they're not going to be so much safer that

00:17:33.593 --> 00:17:36.610
that's going to be the reason you adopt.

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:38.210
It's not gonna be because they get you

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:39.480
to the location faster.

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:41.430
Everything we see with autonomy is

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:42.950
they're going to be slower

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:46.910
until majority of the fleet is autonomous.

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.560
They're cautious and therefore slower

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:51.670
and therefore more annoying
in the way we think

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:53.730
about actually how we navigate this world.

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:56.230
We take risk, we drive
assertively with speed

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:57.810
over the speed limit all the time.

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:00.510
That is not how autonomous
vehicles today operate.

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:02.280
So they're not gonna get us there faster

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.220
and for every promise,

00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:06.800
every hope that they're
going to be cheaper

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:11.180
really there's still significant
investment going into them

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:14.330
and there is not good
economics in the near term

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:17.423
of how to make them obviously
significantly cheaper.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:23.620
What I think Uber and Lyft has
taken over the taxi service

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:26.340
because of the human experience.

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:29.860
In the same way autonomy
will only take over if,

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:32.440
not take over but be
adopted by human beings

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.860
if it creates a better human experience.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:37.340
If there's something about the experience

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:40.100
that you enjoy the heck out of.

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:44.390
This video and many others
that we're putting out,

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:46.860
shows that in the natural
language communication,

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:48.560
the interaction with the car,

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.940
the ability of the car to
sense everything you're doing

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:55.120
from the activity of the driver
to the driver's attention

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.710
and being able to transfer
control back and forth

00:18:57.710 --> 00:19:01.010
in a playful way but
really in a serious way

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:05.650
also that's personalized to you.

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:08.180
That's really the human experience,

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:10.000
the efficiency of the human experience,

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:11.550
the richness of the human experience,

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:13.613
that is what we need to also solve.

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:15.650
That's something you have to think about

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:16.970
because many of the people,

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:18.870
that'll be speaking at this class

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:20.800
and many of the people that are working

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:24.810
on this problem are not focused
on the human experience.

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:27.030
It's a kind of afterthought

00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:29.280
that once we solve the
autonomous vehicle problem

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:31.553
it'll be fun as hell to be in that car.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.610
I believe you first have to make it fun

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:35.860
as hell to be in the car

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:39.883
and then solve the autonomous
vehicle problem jointly.

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:43.227
So in the language that
we're talking about here

00:19:43.227 --> 00:19:46.540
there are several levels of
autonomy that are defined

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:47.940
from level zero to level four.

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:50.930
Level zero no automation, four and five,

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:53.460
level three, four and five
increasing automation.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:56.890
So level two is when the
driver is still responsible,

00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:58.180
level three, four, five is

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:00.350
when there's less and less responsibility.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:02.410
But really in three, four, five,

00:20:02.410 --> 00:20:04.880
there's parts of the driving

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.230
where the liability's on the car.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:11.200
So there's only really two, as
far as I'm concerned, levels,

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.250
human center autonomy and full autonomy.

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:15.870
Human centered means the
human is responsible.

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:19.510
Full autonomy means the car is responsible

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:23.490
both on the legal side,
the experience side

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:25.940
and the algorithm side.

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:30.940
That means full autonomy does
not allow for teleoperation.

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:35.580
So it doesn't allow for
the human to step in

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:37.040
and remotely control the vehicle

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:39.520
because that means the
human is still in the loop.

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:41.780
It doesn't allow for the 10 second rule

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:44.580
that it's gonna be fully autonomous

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:46.240
but once it starts warning you,

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:47.590
you have 10 seconds to take over.

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:49.460
No, it's not fully autonomous

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:53.970
if it cannot guarantee
safety in any situation.

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:55.000
It has to be able to,

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:57.270
if the driver doesn't
respond in 10 seconds

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:58.890
it has to be able to find safe harbor.

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:01.060
It has to be able to pull
off to the side of the road

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:04.970
without hurting anybody
else to find safety.

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:08.150
So that's the fully autonomous challenge.

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:11.810
And so how do we envision these two levels

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:14.320
of automation proliferating society,

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:16.150
getting deployed at a mass scale?

00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:19.660
The 10,000, 10 million beyond.

00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:21.223
On the fully autonomous side,

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:27.250
the way to think about
it with the predictions

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:29.420
that we're talking about here,

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:31.280
is there's several different possibilities

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:32.943
of how to deploy these vehicles.

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:40.680
One is last mile delivery
of goods and services

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:42.830
like the groceries.

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:45.290
These are zero occupancy
vehicles delivering groceries

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:48.900
or delivering human
beings at the last mile.

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:53.080
What the last mile means is
it's slow-moving transport

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:56.420
to the destination where
most of the tricky driving

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:57.840
along the way is done manually

00:21:57.840 --> 00:21:59.650
and then the last mile
delivery in the city

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:02.150
in the urban environment is done

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:06.710
by zero occupancy autonomous vehicles.

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:10.000
Trucking on the highway,
possibly with platooning,

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:12.350
where a sequence of
trucks follow each other.

00:22:12.350 --> 00:22:14.630
So in this what people think about it

00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:19.630
as a pretty well-defined
problem of highway driving

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:23.810
with lanes well marked, well mapped routes

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:26.470
throughout the United States and globally

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:28.950
on the highway driving is automatable.

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:31.020
The specific urban routes kind of like

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:33.380
what a lot of the these
companies are working on,

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:36.250
defining this taxi service

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:38.450
and personalized public transport.

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:41.610
There's certain pickup locations
you're allowed to go to,

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:43.850
there are certain drop-off
locations and that's it.

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:45.710
It's kind of like taking the train here

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:47.530
but as opposed to getting on the train

00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:50.220
with 100 other people
you're getting or bus,

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:52.080
you're getting on the car with,

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:54.230
when you're alone or
with one other person.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:57.520
The closed communities,

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:00.180
something Oliver Cameron
with Voyage is working on

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:03.120
defining and Optimus Ride,

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:04.770
defining a particular community

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:08.570
that you now have a monopoly over

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:09.720
that you define the constraints,

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:11.050
you define the customer base

00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:12.600
and then you just deliver the vehicles.

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:16.100
You map the entire road, you
have slow-moving transport

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:18.900
that gets people from A to B
anywhere in that community.

00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:24.180
And then there's the world

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:26.800
of zero occupancy ride-sharing delivery.

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:28.250
So the Uber that comes to you

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.540
as opposed to having you drive it yourself

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:33.890
and it comes to you autonomously
with nobody in there

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:36.520
and then you get in and drive it.

00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:37.850
So imagine a world

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:40.920
where we have empty
vehicles driving around,

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.070
delivering themselves to you.

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:47.347
Semi-autonomous side is
thinking about a world

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:51.430
where teleoperation plays
a really crucial role

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:53.570
where it's fully autonomous
under certain constraints

00:23:53.570 --> 00:23:55.870
on the highway but a
human can always step in.

00:23:56.830 --> 00:23:58.530
High autonomy on the highway kind of like

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:02.090
what Tesla is working
towards most recently,

00:24:02.090 --> 00:24:03.950
it's on-ramp to off-ramp.

00:24:03.950 --> 00:24:07.890
Now the driver is still
responsible, liability wise

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:09.680
and in terms of just observing the vehicle

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:11.930
and algorithmically speaking

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:16.190
but the autonomy is pretty high level

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:17.400
to a point where much

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:20.260
of the highway driving could
be done fully autonomously.

00:24:20.260 --> 00:24:23.570
And low autonomy unrestricted travel

00:24:23.570 --> 00:24:27.670
as an advanced driver assistance system,

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:31.730
meaning that the car
kind of like the Tesla,

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:35.260
the Volvo S90s or the Super
Cruise and the Cadillacs

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:37.440
all these kinds of L2
systems that are able

00:24:37.440 --> 00:24:39.030
to keep you in the lane,

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:42.077
you know 10 to 30% of
the miles that you drive

00:24:42.077 --> 00:24:44.420
and some fraction of the time take some

00:24:44.420 --> 00:24:46.250
of the stress of driving off.

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:50.260
And then there is some
out there ideas, right.

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.320
The idea of connected vehicles,

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.100
vehicle to vehicle communication

00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:57.120
and vehicle to infrastructure
communication enabling us

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:00.960
to navigate, for example,
intersection efficiently

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:03.230
without stopping, removing
all traffic lights.

00:25:03.230 --> 00:25:06.590
So here shown on the bottom
is our conventional approach

00:25:06.590 --> 00:25:09.061
of there's a queuing system that forms

00:25:09.061 --> 00:25:12.010
because of traffic lights
that turn red, green, yellow

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:15.440
and without traffic lights
and with communication

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:17.350
to the infrastructure
in between the vehicles

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:18.650
you can actually optimize that

00:25:18.650 --> 00:25:22.790
to significantly increase the
traffic load through a city.

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:26.610
Of course there's the boring solution

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:32.463
of tunnels under cities,

00:25:34.730 --> 00:25:36.463
layers of tunnels under cities.

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:40.113
Tunnels all the way down.

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:45.340
Autonomous vehicles basically

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:47.550
by the design of the tunnel,

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:50.040
constraining the problem to such a degree

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:53.110
that an, I mean the idea of autonomy

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:55.010
just is completely transformed.

00:25:55.010 --> 00:25:58.390
That you're basically, a car
is able to transform itself

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:01.910
into a mini train, into a
mini public transit entity,

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:04.260
for a particular period of time.

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:05.830
So you get into that tunnel,

00:26:05.830 --> 00:26:07.710
you drive at 200 miles an hour

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:11.930
and or not necessarily drive,
be driven 200 miles an hour

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:14.260
and then you get out of the tunnel.

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:16.210
Of course there's the flying cars,

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:18.300
personalized flying car vehicles.

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:20.023
I will not, I mean,

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:26.340
Rodney as I mentioned before, does believe

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:28.420
that we'll have them in 2050.

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:30.720
There's a lot of people that
are seriously actually thinking

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:34.660
about this problem is
there's a level of autonomy

00:26:34.660 --> 00:26:37.963
obviously that's required
here for a regular person.

00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:42.250
I don't know somebody
without a pilot's license,

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:45.330
for example, to be able
to take off and land.

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:49.650
Making that experience accessible
to regular people means

00:26:49.650 --> 00:26:51.080
that there's going to
be a significant amount

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:52.400
of autonomy involved.

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:53.990
One of the people really,

00:26:53.990 --> 00:26:56.980
one of the companies really
seriously working on this,

00:26:56.980 --> 00:27:01.980
is Uber with the Uber Elevate,
Uber Air I think it's called

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:05.700
and the idea is that you
would meet your vehicle

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:08.270
not on the street but at a roof,

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:09.103
you take it elevator,

00:27:09.103 --> 00:27:13.090
you meet them at the roof of a building.

00:27:13.090 --> 00:27:15.170
This video's from Uber.

00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:18.110
They're seriously addressing this problem.

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:20.310
Many of the great solutions

00:27:20.310 --> 00:27:22.250
to the world's problems
have been laughed at

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:23.650
at some point.

00:27:23.650 --> 00:27:28.303
So let's not laugh too loud
at these possibilities.

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:32.630
Back in my day we used
to drive in the street.

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:37.630
Okay so aha, 10,000 vehicles,

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:39.500
if that's the bar.

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:42.060
I sort of out of curiosity asked,

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:43.890
did a little public poll.

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:46.160
3,000 people responded.

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:48.550
Asked who will be first

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:52.240
to deploy 10,000 fully
autonomous cars operating

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:55.623
on public roads without a safety driver.

00:27:56.850 --> 00:28:01.850
And several options percolated
with Tesla getting 57%

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:06.820
of the vote and Waymo
gaining 21% of the vote

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:11.820
and 14% someone else
and 8% the curmudgeons

00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:15.600
and the engineers saying

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:17.803
no one in the next 50 years will do it.

00:28:19.180 --> 00:28:23.160
And again in 1998 when Google came along,

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:26.580
the leaders of the space were Ask Jeeves

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:30.340
and Infoseek and Excite,
all services I've used

00:28:30.340 --> 00:28:32.380
and probably some people
in this room have used,

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:36.750
Lycos, Yahoo, obviously they
were the leaders in the space

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:39.150
and Google disrupted
that space completely.

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:43.510
So this poll shows the current leaders

00:28:43.510 --> 00:28:46.460
but it's wide open to ideas
and that's why there's a lot

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:48.030
of autonomous vehicle companies.

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:51.800
Some companies are taking
advantage of the hype

00:28:51.800 --> 00:28:54.260
and the fact that there's a
lot of investment in the space

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:59.130
but some companies, like
some of the speakers visiting

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:01.420
in this course are really
trying to solve this problem.

00:29:01.420 --> 00:29:02.910
They want to be the next Google,

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:04.860
the next billion, multi-billion,

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:08.060
next trillion dollar company
by solving the problem.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:09.430
So it's wide open.

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:11.950
But currently Tesla with a human,

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:15.840
with the semi-autonomous
vehicle approach working

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:18.720
towards trying to become fully autonomous.

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:21.180
And Waymo starting with the
fully autonomous working

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:23.690
towards achieving scale
at the fully autonomous

00:29:23.690 --> 00:29:25.810
are the leaders in the space.

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:27.083
Given that,

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:31.190
ranking in 2019,

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:33.520
let's take a quick step back to 2005

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:36.540
with the DARPA challenge
when the story began.

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:40.143
The race to the desert when
Stanley from Stanford won a race

00:29:40.143 --> 00:29:42.240
through the desert

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:44.470
that really captivated
people's imagination

00:29:44.470 --> 00:29:45.680
about what's possible.

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:47.010
And a lot of people have said

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:50.380
that the autonomous vehicle
problem is solved in 2005.

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:53.350
They really said you know
the idea was especially

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:55.780
because in 2004 nobody finished that race,

00:29:55.780 --> 00:29:58.170
2005 four cars finished the race,

00:29:58.170 --> 00:29:59.730
it was like well we cracked it.

00:29:59.730 --> 00:30:01.260
This is it.

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:03.730
And then you know some critics said

00:30:03.730 --> 00:30:07.620
that urban driving is
really nothing comparable

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:10.270
to desert driving, desert is very simple

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:11.840
there's no obstacles and so on.

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:13.690
It's really a mechanical
engineering problem

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:14.840
it's not a software problem.

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:16.720
It's not a fundamentally,

00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:18.500
it's not really an
autonomous driving problem

00:30:18.500 --> 00:30:20.460
as it would be delivered to consumers

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:22.149
and of course in 2007,

00:30:22.149 --> 00:30:24.590
DARPA put together Urban Grand Challenge

00:30:24.590 --> 00:30:28.263
and several people finished
that with CMU's boss winning.

00:30:29.819 --> 00:30:31.680
And so the thought was at that point,

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:33.270
that's it, we're done.

00:30:33.270 --> 00:30:37.210
As Ernest Rutherford, a physicist, said,

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:38.980
that physics is the only real science,

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:40.460
the rest is just stamp collecting,

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:42.050
all the biology, chemistry.

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:43.680
Certainly, oh boy, I wouldn't want to know

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:46.285
what he thinks about computer science.

00:30:46.285 --> 00:30:48.300
It's just all this stupid silly details

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:49.530
Physics is the fundamentals.

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:51.490
And that was the idea

00:30:51.490 --> 00:30:53.670
with the DARPA Grand
Challenge and solving that

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:56.430
that we solved the fundamental
problem of autonomy.

00:30:56.430 --> 00:30:59.640
And the rest is just for
industry to figure out

00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:02.020
some of the details of how to make an app

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:04.193
and make a business out of it.

00:31:05.070 --> 00:31:06.230
So that could be true.

00:31:06.230 --> 00:31:07.770
And the underlying beliefs there is

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:12.160
that driving is an easy
task, that it's solvable.

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:13.720
The thing that we do as human beings

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:16.020
that it's pretty formalizable

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:19.940
it's pretty easy to solve with autonomy

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:23.030
that the other idea is that
humans are bad at driving.

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:24.570
This is a common belief.

00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:27.640
Not me, not you but everybody else,

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:30.178
nobody in this room but everybody
else is a terrible driver.

00:31:30.178 --> 00:31:32.600
The kind of intuition that we have

00:31:32.600 --> 00:31:34.780
about our experience of
traffic leads us to believe

00:31:34.780 --> 00:31:37.070
that humans are just
really bad at driving.

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:40.850
And from the human
factors, psychology side,

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:45.850
there's been over 70 years

00:31:48.218 --> 00:31:52.407
of research showing
that humans are not able

00:31:53.560 --> 00:31:57.230
to monitor, maintain
vigilance, monitoring a system.

00:31:57.230 --> 00:32:00.560
So when you put a human
in a room with a robot

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:03.639
and say watch that robot,

00:32:03.639 --> 00:32:07.840
they start texting like 15 seconds in.

00:32:07.840 --> 00:32:09.930
So that's the fundamental psychology.

00:32:09.930 --> 00:32:11.860
There's thousands of papers on this.

00:32:11.860 --> 00:32:14.740
People are, they tune out,
they over trust the system,

00:32:14.740 --> 00:32:19.120
they misinterpret the system
and they lose vigilance.

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:21.110
Those are the three underlying beliefs.

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:25.780
It very well could be true
but what if it is not?

00:32:25.780 --> 00:32:28.660
So we have to consider that it is not.

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:30.420
The driving task is easy

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:32.300
because if you think
the driving task is easy

00:32:32.300 --> 00:32:34.440
and formalizable and solvable
by autonomous vehicles,

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:36.450
you have to solve this problem.

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:38.910
The subtle vehicle-to-vehicle,

00:32:38.910 --> 00:32:41.290
vehicles-to-pedestrian
nonverbal communication

00:32:41.290 --> 00:32:44.820
that happens here in a dramatic sense

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:46.887
but really happens in the subtle sense

00:32:46.887 --> 00:32:50.770
millions of times every
single day in Boston.

00:32:50.770 --> 00:32:53.970
Subtle nonverbal communication
between vehicles,

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:55.403
you go, no, you go.

00:32:56.510 --> 00:33:00.140
You have to solve all
the crazy road conditions

00:33:00.140 --> 00:33:04.100
where in a split seconds
you have to make a decision

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:07.920
about, so in snowy, icy weather, rain,

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:09.680
limited visibility conditions,

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:13.270
you have 100, 200 milliseconds
to make a decision.

00:33:13.270 --> 00:33:15.110
Your algorithm based on the perception has

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:16.460
to make a control decision.

00:33:19.250 --> 00:33:20.083
And then you have to deal

00:33:20.083 --> 00:33:22.650
with a nonverbal communication
with pedestrians,

00:33:22.650 --> 00:33:26.820
these unreasonable irrational
creatures, us human beings.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:29.040
You have to not only understand

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:34.040
what the intent of the
movement that's anticipated.

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:37.210
So anticipating the
trajectory of the pedestrian

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:40.450
you also have to assert
yourself in a game theoretic way

00:33:40.450 --> 00:33:43.220
as crazy as it might sound,
you have to threaten yourself,

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:45.060
you have to take a risk.

00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:46.890
You have to take a risk that

00:33:46.890 --> 00:33:50.330
if I don't slow down like that
ambulance didn't slow down

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:52.813
that the pedestrian will slow down.

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:57.143
Algorithmically we're afraid to do that.

00:33:58.280 --> 00:34:02.360
The idea that a pedestrian that's moving,

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:05.090
we anticipate their trajectory
based on the simple physics

00:34:05.090 --> 00:34:06.780
of the current velocity of the momentum,

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:09.460
they're gonna keep going
with some probability.

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:12.650
The fact that by us accelerating

00:34:12.650 --> 00:34:15.090
we might make that pedestrian stop,

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:17.300
it's something that we have
to incorporate into algorithms

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:18.583
and we don't today.

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:21.280
And we don't know how to really.

00:34:22.821 --> 00:34:24.620
So if driving is easy we
have to solve that too.

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:26.350
And of course the thing I showed yesterday

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:28.840
with the coast runners
and the boat going around

00:34:31.050 --> 00:34:36.050
and all the ethical dilemmas
from the moral machine

00:34:36.240 --> 00:34:40.067
to the more serious engineering aspects

00:34:40.067 --> 00:34:43.510
that from the unintended consequences

00:34:43.510 --> 00:34:48.510
that arise from having to
formalize the objective function

00:34:49.330 --> 00:34:52.880
under which a planning algorithm operates.

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:56.000
If there's any learning
that, as I showed yesterday,

00:34:56.000 --> 00:34:59.450
a boat on the left run by a
human wants to finish the race,

00:34:59.450 --> 00:35:00.920
the boat on the right figures out

00:35:00.920 --> 00:35:02.460
that it doesn't have to finish the race,

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:04.750
it can pick up turbos along the way

00:35:04.750 --> 00:35:06.640
and gets much more reward.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:09.080
So if the objective function
is to maximize the reward,

00:35:09.080 --> 00:35:11.540
you can slam into the wall
over and over and over again

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:14.380
and that's actually the
way to optimize the reward.

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:17.980
And those are the unintended
consequences of an algorithm

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:21.100
that has to be formalizable
to the objective function

00:35:21.100 --> 00:35:22.500
without a human in the loop.

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:24.960
Humans are bad at driving.

00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:26.343
As I showed yesterday,

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:30.840
humans if they're bad at anything

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:32.400
it's about having a good intuition

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:36.320
about what's hard and what's easy.

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:38.830
The fact that we have 540
million years worth of data

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:41.090
on our visual perception system means

00:35:41.090 --> 00:35:43.960
we don't understand how
damn impressive it is

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:46.170
to be able to perceive
and understand the scene

00:35:46.170 --> 00:35:49.010
in a split second, maintain context,

00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:51.760
maintain an understanding of performing

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:54.620
all the visual localization tasks

00:35:54.620 --> 00:35:58.153
about anticipating the physics
of the scene and so on.

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:01.060
And then there's a control side.

00:36:01.060 --> 00:36:04.130
The humans don't give
enough credit to ourselves.

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:09.130
We're incredible, state-of-the-art
soccer player on the left

00:36:09.911 --> 00:36:10.744
(audience laughing)

00:36:10.744 --> 00:36:13.413
and the state-of-the-art
robot on the right.

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:29.997
I think there's like four
or five times he scores,

00:36:29.997 --> 00:36:32.933
(audience laughing)
all right.

00:36:34.210 --> 00:36:36.680
And this is all the movement
and so on involved with that,

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:39.560
of course here that's the human robot,

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:41.017
that's a really incredible work

00:36:41.017 --> 00:36:43.400
that's done for the
DARPA Robotics Challenge

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:47.580
with the humanoid robots on
the right and incredible work

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:52.580
by the human people doing
the same kind of tasks

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:55.090
much more impressive task I would say.

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:56.825
So that's where we stand.

00:36:56.825 --> 00:36:59.053
And the ones on the right are
actually not fully autonomous,

00:36:59.053 --> 00:37:01.150
there's still some human in the loop.

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:03.600
There's just noisy broken communication.

00:37:03.600 --> 00:37:05.270
So that, humans are incredible

00:37:05.270 --> 00:37:07.710
in terms of our ability
to understand the world

00:37:07.710 --> 00:37:10.400
and in terms of our ability
to act in that world.

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:14.420
And the fact that humans,
the idea, the view,

00:37:14.420 --> 00:37:18.010
the popular view grounded in
the psychology that humans

00:37:18.010 --> 00:37:21.460
and automations don't
mix well, over trust,

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:24.693
misunderstanding, loss of
vigilance, the command and so on,

00:37:25.590 --> 00:37:27.680
that's not an obvious fact.

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:29.110
It happens a lot in the lab.

00:37:29.110 --> 00:37:31.890
Most of the experiments
are actually in the lab.

00:37:31.890 --> 00:37:33.866
This is the difference.

00:37:33.866 --> 00:37:38.730
You put, many of you, you
put a undergrad, grad student

00:37:38.730 --> 00:37:41.610
in a lab and say here watch this screen

00:37:41.610 --> 00:37:44.100
and wait for the dot to appear.

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:47.080
They'll tune out immediately
but when it's your life

00:37:47.080 --> 00:37:50.530
and you're on the road,
it's just you in the car,

00:37:50.530 --> 00:37:51.670
it's a different experience.

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:54.520
It's not completely obvious
that vigilance will be lost

00:37:54.520 --> 00:37:58.530
and it's not a complete, when
it's just you and the robot,

00:37:58.530 --> 00:38:02.070
it's not completely obvious
what the psychology,

00:38:02.070 --> 00:38:03.460
what the attentional mechanism,

00:38:03.460 --> 00:38:05.270
with the vigilance that it looks like.

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:06.310
So one of the things we did,

00:38:06.310 --> 00:38:09.760
is we instrumented here 22
Tesla's and observed people

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:12.560
now over a period of two
years of what they actually do

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:15.570
when they're driving autopilot,
driving these systems.

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:17.540
In red shown manually controlled vehicles

00:38:17.540 --> 00:38:22.050
and cyan showed vehicle control autopilot.

00:38:22.050 --> 00:38:23.640
Now there's a lot of details here

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:25.320
and we have a lot of presentations on this

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:26.910
but really, the fundamentals are,

00:38:26.910 --> 00:38:29.850
is that they drive 34%,

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:32.690
large percentage of the miles in autopilot

00:38:32.690 --> 00:38:37.690
and in 26,000 moments
of transfer of control

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:42.290
they are always vigilant.

00:38:42.290 --> 00:38:45.790
There's not a moment once in this data set

00:38:45.790 --> 00:38:50.790
where they respond too late
to a critical situation,

00:38:50.830 --> 00:38:52.650
to a challenging role situation.

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:55.760
Now the data set, 22 vehicles,

00:38:55.760 --> 00:39:00.760
that's a 0.1% or less
than the full Tesla fleet

00:39:01.580 --> 00:39:03.010
that has autopilot.

00:39:03.010 --> 00:39:04.470
But it's still an inkling.

00:39:04.470 --> 00:39:06.880
It's not obvious that it's
not possible to build a system

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:08.780
that works together with a human being

00:39:11.140 --> 00:39:14.060
and that system essentially
looks like this.

00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:17.493
Some percentage, 90%,
maybe less, maybe more,

00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:21.160
when it can solve the
problem of autonomous driving

00:39:21.160 --> 00:39:24.430
it solves it and when he needs
human help it asks for help.

00:39:24.430 --> 00:39:27.130
That's the trade-off, that's the balance.

00:39:27.130 --> 00:39:28.570
On the fully autonomous side,

00:39:28.570 --> 00:39:32.330
on the right it has to
solve here with citations

00:39:32.330 --> 00:39:35.170
and there's references
always on the bottom.

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:38.950
All the problems have to be
solved exceptionally, perfectly,

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:41.580
from mapping localization
to the scene perception

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:46.580
to control to planning to
being able to find safe harbor

00:39:46.880 --> 00:39:48.810
at any moment to also being able

00:39:48.810 --> 00:39:51.860
to do external HMI communication
with the other pedestrians,

00:39:51.860 --> 00:39:53.170
the vehicles in the scene

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:54.560
and then there's teleoperation,

00:39:54.560 --> 00:39:56.380
vehicle-to-vehicle, vehicle-to-AI.

00:39:56.380 --> 00:39:58.010
You have to solve those perfectly

00:39:58.010 --> 00:40:00.470
if you want to solve the
fully autonomous problem,

00:40:00.470 --> 00:40:02.690
as I said including all the crazy things

00:40:02.690 --> 00:40:04.420
that happen in driving.

00:40:04.420 --> 00:40:06.880
And if you approach the
shared autonomy side,

00:40:06.880 --> 00:40:09.670
the semi-autonomous where
you're only responsible

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:12.320
for a large percentage but
not 100% of the driving

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:14.680
then you have to solve the human side,

00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:18.140
the human interaction, the
sensing what the driver is doing,

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:20.850
the collaborating
communicating with the driver

00:40:20.850 --> 00:40:23.800
and the personalization aspect
that learns with the driver.

00:40:25.745 --> 00:40:29.250
As I said you can go online,
we have a lot of demonstrations

00:40:29.250 --> 00:40:30.740
of these kinds of ideas.

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:33.710
But the natural language,
the communication,

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:35.480
I think is critical for all of us

00:40:35.480 --> 00:40:37.333
as we're tweeting as all of us do.

00:40:43.609 --> 00:40:44.940
(people chattering)

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:46.730
So it's as simple as,

00:40:46.730 --> 00:40:49.010
so this is just demonstration
of Eco taking control

00:40:49.010 --> 00:40:52.871
when the attention over time,

00:40:52.871 --> 00:40:54.663
that the driver is being,

00:40:56.940 --> 00:40:58.240
okay, we got it thank you.

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:05.330
Okay so basically a smartphone use

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:06.640
which has gone up year by year

00:41:06.640 --> 00:41:07.983
and we're doing a lot of analysis on that,

00:41:07.983 --> 00:41:09.480
it's really what people do

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:11.593
in the car is they use their phone,

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:14.490
whether it's manual or autonomous driving

00:41:14.490 --> 00:41:15.640
or semi-autonomous driving.

00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:19.700
So being able to manage that,
to communicate with the driver

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:22.570
about when they should be paying attention

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:24.030
which may not be always.

00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:27.180
You're sort of balancing the
time when it's a critical time

00:41:27.180 --> 00:41:28.640
to pay attention when it's not

00:41:28.640 --> 00:41:31.630
and communicating effectively,
learning with the driver,

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:35.370
that problem is a fundamental
machine learning problem.

00:41:35.370 --> 00:41:37.580
There's a lot of data visible light,

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:40.660
everything about the driver
and it's a psychology problem.

00:41:40.660 --> 00:41:44.730
So we have data, we have
complicated human beings

00:41:44.730 --> 00:41:47.390
and it's a human robot interaction problem

00:41:47.390 --> 00:41:48.633
that deserves solving.

00:41:49.570 --> 00:41:54.570
But as you'll hear on the beyond
the human side looking out

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:56.780
into the world, people that are trying

00:41:56.780 --> 00:41:58.160
to solve the fully autonomous vehicle

00:41:58.160 --> 00:42:00.910
it's really a two approach consideration.

00:42:00.910 --> 00:42:05.910
One approach is vision, cameras
and deep learning, right.

00:42:07.690 --> 00:42:09.950
Collect a huge amount of data.

00:42:09.950 --> 00:42:13.230
So cameras have this aspect

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:14.870
that they're the highest resolution

00:42:14.870 --> 00:42:16.300
of information available.

00:42:16.300 --> 00:42:18.750
It's rich texture information

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:21.370
and there's a lot of it which is exactly

00:42:21.370 --> 00:42:22.960
what you know networks love right.

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:26.887
So to be able to cover
all the crazy edge cases,

00:42:26.887 --> 00:42:30.630
the vision data, camera
data, visible light data,

00:42:30.630 --> 00:42:32.400
is exactly the kind of data you need

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:34.840
to collect a huge amount of,
to be able to generalize over

00:42:34.840 --> 00:42:37.373
all the crazy countless
edge cases that happen.

00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:40.800
It's also feasible, all
the major data sets,

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:45.390
all the, in terms of
cost, interest, scale,

00:42:45.390 --> 00:42:48.690
all the major data sets
of visible light cameras.

00:42:48.690 --> 00:42:50.680
That's another pro and they're cheap

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:53.383
and the world as it happens,

00:42:54.350 --> 00:42:56.770
whoever designed the simulation
that we're all living in,

00:42:56.770 --> 00:43:01.770
made it such that our world,
our roads and our world,

00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:04.783
is designed for human eyes.

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:09.407
Eyes is the way we perceive the world

00:43:09.407 --> 00:43:11.690
and so the lane mark is also on is visual,

00:43:11.690 --> 00:43:14.924
most of the road textures that you use

00:43:14.924 --> 00:43:19.924
to navigate, to drive are
visible, are made for human eyes.

00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:24.800
The cons are that without a ton of data

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:29.070
and we don't know how
much, they're not accurate.

00:43:29.070 --> 00:43:30.870
You make errors because
driving is ultimately

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:35.870
about 99.99999% accuracy
and so that's what I mean

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:37.250
by not accurate.

00:43:37.250 --> 00:43:40.433
It's really difficult to reach that level.

00:43:41.810 --> 00:43:44.203
And then the second approach is LIDAR,

00:43:45.170 --> 00:43:48.890
taking a very particular
constrained set of roads,

00:43:48.890 --> 00:43:51.100
mapping the heck out of them,

00:43:51.100 --> 00:43:52.190
understanding them fully

00:43:52.190 --> 00:43:54.150
under different weather
conditions and so on

00:43:54.150 --> 00:43:58.040
and then using the most
accurate sensors available.

00:43:58.040 --> 00:44:01.610
A suite one sensors but
really LIDAR at the forefront.

00:44:01.610 --> 00:44:03.900
Being able to localize
yourself effectively.

00:44:03.900 --> 00:44:06.580
The pros there that it's consistent,

00:44:06.580 --> 00:44:09.110
especially when machine
learning is not involved,

00:44:09.110 --> 00:44:12.640
it's consistent and reliable
and it's explainable.

00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:14.930
If it fails, you can understand why,

00:44:14.930 --> 00:44:16.650
you can account for those situations.

00:44:16.650 --> 00:44:19.910
It's not so much true for
machine learning methods.

00:44:19.910 --> 00:44:21.950
It's not so much explainable why it failed

00:44:21.950 --> 00:44:23.980
in a particular situation.

00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:26.320
The accuracy is higher
as we'll talk about.

00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:29.320
The cons of LIDAR is that it's expensive

00:44:29.320 --> 00:44:31.730
and most of the approaches

00:44:31.730 --> 00:44:35.650
in perceiving the world
using LIDAR primarily

00:44:35.650 --> 00:44:37.280
are not deep learning based

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:39.540
and therefore they're
not learning over time.

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:41.210
And if they were deep learning based,

00:44:41.210 --> 00:44:42.360
there's a reason they're not,

00:44:42.360 --> 00:44:45.700
it's 'cause you need a lot of car,

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:47.880
you gonna need a lot of LIDAR data.

00:44:47.880 --> 00:44:51.610
And there's only a tiny
percentage of cars in the world

00:44:51.610 --> 00:44:55.620
quite obviously are equipped with LIDAR

00:44:55.620 --> 00:44:57.900
in order to collect that data.

00:44:57.900 --> 00:45:00.920
So quickly running through the sensors.

00:45:00.920 --> 00:45:05.920
Radar is, it's kind of like
the offensive line of football.

00:45:06.940 --> 00:45:08.700
They're actually the
ones that do all the work

00:45:08.700 --> 00:45:10.940
and they never get the credit.

00:45:10.940 --> 00:45:12.540
So radar is that.

00:45:12.540 --> 00:45:14.810
It's always behind to catch,

00:45:14.810 --> 00:45:17.830
to actually do the detection
in terms of obstacle,

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:20.910
the most critical safety
critical obstacle avoidance.

00:45:20.910 --> 00:45:23.010
It's cheap, it does extremely well

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:25.830
and it does well in extreme weather

00:45:25.830 --> 00:45:30.133
but it's low resolution so
it cannot stand on its own

00:45:30.133 --> 00:45:33.330
to achieve any kind of
degree of high autonomy.

00:45:33.330 --> 00:45:35.580
Now on the LIDAR side it's expensive,

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:37.460
it's extremely accurate depth information,

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:39.850
3D cloud, point cloud information.

00:45:39.850 --> 00:45:42.730
Its resolution is much higher than radar

00:45:42.730 --> 00:45:45.030
but still lower than visible light

00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:47.240
and there is depending on the sensor,

00:45:47.240 --> 00:45:50.533
a 360 degree visibility that's built in.

00:45:51.480 --> 00:45:53.740
So there's a difference
in resolution here,

00:45:53.740 --> 00:45:57.940
visualized LIDAR on the
right, radar on the left.

00:45:57.940 --> 00:46:00.260
The resolution is just much
higher and is improving

00:46:00.260 --> 00:46:02.130
and the cost is going down and so on.

00:46:02.130 --> 00:46:05.740
Now on the camera side, it's
cheap, everybody got one,

00:46:05.740 --> 00:46:07.130
the resolution is extremely high

00:46:07.130 --> 00:46:11.560
in terms of the amount of
information transferred per frame

00:46:11.560 --> 00:46:16.050
and everybody you know really the scale

00:46:16.050 --> 00:46:17.300
of the number of vehicles

00:46:17.300 --> 00:46:19.890
that have this equipped is humongous.

00:46:19.890 --> 00:46:24.890
So it's ripe for application
of deep learning.

00:46:24.980 --> 00:46:27.830
And the challenge is it's noisy,

00:46:27.830 --> 00:46:29.320
it's bad at depth estimation

00:46:30.520 --> 00:46:32.853
and it's not good in extreme weather.

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:38.800
So if we kind use this plot to look,

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:40.380
to compare these sensors,

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:42.510
to compare these different approaches.

00:46:42.510 --> 00:46:45.680
So LIDAR works in the dark,

00:46:45.680 --> 00:46:48.410
variable lighting conditions,
has pretty good resolution,

00:46:48.410 --> 00:46:52.693
has pretty good range but it's expensive,

00:46:53.700 --> 00:46:55.860
it's huge,

00:46:55.860 --> 00:47:00.860
and it doesn't provide rich
textural contrast information

00:47:01.860 --> 00:47:04.430
and it's also sensitive to
fog and rain conditions.

00:47:04.430 --> 00:47:08.140
Now ultrasonic sensors catch
a lot of those problems.

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:09.850
They're better at detecting proximity,

00:47:09.850 --> 00:47:13.370
they're high resolution
in objects that are close

00:47:13.370 --> 00:47:15.030
which is why they're
often used for parking

00:47:15.030 --> 00:47:16.640
but they can still also be integrated

00:47:16.640 --> 00:47:19.330
in the sensor fusion package

00:47:19.330 --> 00:47:20.720
for an autonomous vehicle.

00:47:20.720 --> 00:47:25.110
They really catch a lot of
the problems that radar has.

00:47:25.110 --> 00:47:27.300
They complement each other well

00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:32.300
and radar, cheap, tiny, detect speed

00:47:33.980 --> 00:47:38.320
and has pretty good range

00:47:38.320 --> 00:47:42.290
but has terrible resolution.

00:47:42.290 --> 00:47:44.640
There's very little
information being provided.

00:47:45.620 --> 00:47:50.600
And then cameras a lot
of rich information,

00:47:50.600 --> 00:47:53.600
they're are cheap, their
small range is great,

00:47:53.600 --> 00:47:56.230
the best range actually of all the sensors

00:47:56.230 --> 00:47:59.770
and works in bright conditions

00:47:59.770 --> 00:48:01.910
but doesn't work in the
dark, in extreme conditions

00:48:01.910 --> 00:48:04.980
and it's just susceptible to
all these kinds of problems

00:48:04.980 --> 00:48:06.320
and doesn't detect speed

00:48:06.320 --> 00:48:09.320
unless you do some tricky structure

00:48:09.320 --> 00:48:10.630
from motion kind of things.

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:12.820
So here's where sense of fusion steps in

00:48:12.820 --> 00:48:15.534
and you, everybody works together

00:48:15.534 --> 00:48:17.741
to build an entire picture.

00:48:17.741 --> 00:48:19.310
That's how this plot works.

00:48:19.310 --> 00:48:21.020
You can stack it on top of each other.

00:48:21.020 --> 00:48:23.710
So if you look at a suite that
for example Tesla is using

00:48:23.710 --> 00:48:25.970
which is ultrasonic radar and camera

00:48:25.970 --> 00:48:27.960
and you compare it to just LIDAR

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:29.940
and see how these paths compare

00:48:29.940 --> 00:48:34.300
that actually the suite of camera, radar

00:48:34.300 --> 00:48:37.163
and ultrasonic are comparable to LIDAR.

00:48:38.140 --> 00:48:40.630
So that those are the two
comparisons that we have.

00:48:40.630 --> 00:48:43.610
You have the costly non
machine-learning way

00:48:43.610 --> 00:48:47.400
of LIDAR and you have the cheap

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:51.970
but needs a lot of data and is
not explainable and reliable

00:48:51.970 --> 00:48:54.800
in the near-term vision based approach.

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:56.520
And those are the two
competing approaches.

00:48:56.520 --> 00:48:58.590
Now of course huevos will
talk about they're trying

00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:02.170
to use both but ultimately the question is

00:49:02.170 --> 00:49:07.170
who catches, who is the fail safe?

00:49:07.900 --> 00:49:09.700
In the semi-autonomous way

00:49:09.700 --> 00:49:11.080
when there's a camera based method,

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:12.950
the human is the fail safe.

00:49:12.950 --> 00:49:15.880
When you say, oh crap I
don't know what to do,

00:49:15.880 --> 00:49:17.710
the human catches.

00:49:17.710 --> 00:49:20.540
In the fully autonomous mode,

00:49:20.540 --> 00:49:24.040
so what Waymo's working on and others,

00:49:24.040 --> 00:49:26.170
the fail safe is LIDAR,

00:49:26.170 --> 00:49:30.570
the fail safe is maps that
you can't rely on the human.

00:49:30.570 --> 00:49:32.700
But you know this road so well

00:49:32.700 --> 00:49:34.300
that if the camera is freaked out

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:36.190
if there's any of the sensors freaked out

00:49:36.190 --> 00:49:39.490
that you're able to,
you have such good maps,

00:49:39.490 --> 00:49:41.260
you have such good accurate sensors,

00:49:41.260 --> 00:49:44.120
that the fundamental problem
of obstacle avoidance

00:49:44.120 --> 00:49:49.120
which is what safety is
about, can be solved.

00:49:49.660 --> 00:49:52.450
The question is what kind
of experience that creates.

00:49:52.450 --> 00:49:54.580
In the meantime as the people debate,

00:49:54.580 --> 00:49:56.900
try to make money, start companies,

00:49:56.900 --> 00:49:58.650
there's just lots of data.

00:49:58.650 --> 00:50:03.240
Ford F-150 still the most
popular car in America.

00:50:03.240 --> 00:50:04.940
Manually driven cars are still happening.

00:50:04.940 --> 00:50:06.240
So there's a lot of data happening.

00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:10.350
Semi-autonomous cars, every
company is now releasing

00:50:10.350 --> 00:50:13.210
more and more semi-autonomous technology.

00:50:13.210 --> 00:50:15.460
So that's all data.

00:50:15.460 --> 00:50:18.380
And what that boils
down to is the two paths

00:50:18.380 --> 00:50:21.880
they're walking towards
is vision versus LIDAR,

00:50:21.880 --> 00:50:25.870
L2 versus L4, semi-autonomous
versus fully autonomous.

00:50:25.870 --> 00:50:28.120
Tesla on the semi-autonomous
front has reached

00:50:28.120 --> 00:50:29.590
one billion miles.

00:50:29.590 --> 00:50:31.455
Waymo the leader on the
autonomous front has reached

00:50:31.455 --> 00:50:33.150
10 million miles.

00:50:33.150 --> 00:50:35.390
The pros and cons as I've outlined them.

00:50:35.390 --> 00:50:40.150
One, division one, the one I'm
obviously very excited about

00:50:40.150 --> 00:50:43.010
and machine learning
researchers excited are about

00:50:43.010 --> 00:50:46.810
which fundamentally relies on
huge data and deep learning.

00:50:46.810 --> 00:50:51.640
The neural networks that
are running inside the Tesla

00:50:51.640 --> 00:50:54.000
and with their new as they,

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:56.210
it's kind of the same kind
of path as Google was taking

00:50:56.210 --> 00:50:58.520
from the GPU to the TPU,

00:50:58.520 --> 00:51:02.060
Tesla's taking from
Nvidia Drive PX2 system,

00:51:02.060 --> 00:51:04.030
sort of more general GPU based system

00:51:04.030 --> 00:51:05.360
to creating their own ASIC

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:07.870
and having a ton of awesome
neural networks running

00:51:07.870 --> 00:51:08.703
on their car.

00:51:08.703 --> 00:51:11.250
That kind of path, that others
are beginning to embrace,

00:51:11.250 --> 00:51:13.750
is really interesting to think about

00:51:13.750 --> 00:51:15.030
for machine learning engineers.

00:51:15.030 --> 00:51:19.070
And then people that
are maybe more grounded

00:51:19.070 --> 00:51:24.070
and actually wanna, are really,
value, safety, reliability

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:28.723
and sort of from the automotive
world, are thinking well

00:51:28.723 --> 00:51:31.110
we need machine learning
is not explainable

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:35.320
it's difficult to work
with, it's not reliable

00:51:35.320 --> 00:51:37.940
and so in that sense we
have to have a sensor suite

00:51:37.940 --> 00:51:38.850
that are extremely reliable.

00:51:38.850 --> 00:51:40.083
Those are the two paths.

00:51:41.530 --> 00:51:43.360
Yep, question.

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:48.160
The question is there's all
kinds of things you need

00:51:48.160 --> 00:51:50.970
to perceive, stop signs
and traffic lights,

00:51:50.970 --> 00:51:52.080
pedestrians and so on.

00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:55.510
Some of them, if you
hit them it's a problem,

00:51:55.510 --> 00:51:58.570
some of them are a bag
flying through the air

00:51:58.570 --> 00:52:00.410
and all have different
visual characteristics

00:52:00.410 --> 00:52:01.640
all have different characteristics

00:52:01.640 --> 00:52:03.580
for all the different sensors.

00:52:03.580 --> 00:52:08.580
So LIDAR can detect of solid-body objects,

00:52:08.740 --> 00:52:10.433
camera is better at detecting,

00:52:11.523 --> 00:52:14.310
as last year Sasha Arnu talked about,

00:52:14.310 --> 00:52:16.600
I think fog or smoke.

00:52:16.600 --> 00:52:17.860
These are interesting things.

00:52:17.860 --> 00:52:19.430
They might look like an object

00:52:19.430 --> 00:52:21.480
to certain sensors and not to others,

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:26.480
But the traffic light
detection problem luckily is

00:52:26.930 --> 00:52:30.770
with cameras is, it's
pretty solved at this point.

00:52:30.770 --> 00:52:33.910
So that's luckily the easy part.

00:52:33.910 --> 00:52:36.130
The hard part is when
you have a green light

00:52:36.130 --> 00:52:41.130
and there's a drunk, drugged,
drowsy or distracted,

00:52:41.370 --> 00:52:44.270
the four Ds that hits an online
pedestrian trying to cross

00:52:45.160 --> 00:52:46.600
what to do.

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:47.700
That's the hard part.

00:52:47.700 --> 00:52:51.110
So the road ahead for us as engineers,

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:52.810
the science is the thing I'm super excited

00:52:52.810 --> 00:52:54.220
about the possibility

00:52:54.220 --> 00:52:56.030
of artificial intelligence
having a huge impact,

00:52:56.030 --> 00:53:00.090
is taking the step from having these

00:53:01.410 --> 00:53:04.380
even if they're large, toy datasets,

00:53:04.380 --> 00:53:08.680
toy problems, toy benchmarks
of ImageNet classification

00:53:09.640 --> 00:53:12.780
in cocoa, all the exciting deep RL stuff

00:53:12.780 --> 00:53:15.050
that we'll talk about in the future weeks,

00:53:15.050 --> 00:53:18.340
really are toy examples, the
game of go and chess and so on.

00:53:18.340 --> 00:53:20.940
But taking those algorithms
and putting them in cars

00:53:20.940 --> 00:53:22.720
where they can save people's lives

00:53:22.720 --> 00:53:24.610
and they actually directly touch

00:53:24.610 --> 00:53:27.550
and impact our entire civilization

00:53:27.550 --> 00:53:29.230
that's actually the defining problem

00:53:29.230 --> 00:53:33.280
for artificial intelligence
in the 21st century is AI

00:53:33.280 --> 00:53:34.930
that touches people in a real way

00:53:34.930 --> 00:53:37.010
and I think cars, autonomous vehicles,

00:53:37.010 --> 00:53:39.440
is one of the big ways that that happens.

00:53:39.440 --> 00:53:42.700
We get to deal with the
psychology, the philosophy,

00:53:42.700 --> 00:53:45.100
the sociology aspects
of it, how we associate,

00:53:45.100 --> 00:53:47.540
think about it, to the robotics problem,

00:53:47.540 --> 00:53:48.810
to the perception problem.

00:53:48.810 --> 00:53:51.000
It's a fascinating space to explore

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:54.670
and we have many guest speakers exploring

00:53:54.670 --> 00:53:55.510
that different ways

00:53:55.510 --> 00:53:58.850
and that's really exciting to see

00:53:58.850 --> 00:54:01.280
how these people are
trying to change the world.

